<wpt lat="49.269400" lon="-0.196500"><name>DZC Merville Battery</name><desc>Tour day 5

The 9th Parachute Battalion attacked this battery despite severe dispersal and lost equipment — the direct parallel to Brécourt. Preserved casemates let you read it from the defender's side.

End inside the defensive landscape and compare with Brécourt on Day 1.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Blue</sym><type>Tour stops</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="49.304960" lon="-1.192860"><name>DZC Normandy Victory Museum</name><desc>Extra stop

Hedgerow fighting and the daily life of soldiers and civilians — the world Easy lived in from 13 to 28 June.

Strong on the bocage battle that followed Carentan.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Blue</sym><type>Tour stops</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="49.175310" lon="-0.441300"><name>DZC D-Day Wings Museum</name><desc>Extra stop

Aviation context — the aircraft and crews that carried the airborne divisions and covered the beaches.

Best before the Caen Memorial on the last day.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Blue</sym><type>Tour stops</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="49.479920" lon="-1.296720"><name>DZC Crisbecq (Marcouf) Battery</name><desc>Tour day 1

Heavy 210 mm naval battery that dueled with the fleet off Utah and was not taken until 12 June. Shows the scale of gun the Brécourt battery was a small cousin of.

Casemates and museum north of Utah Beach.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Blue</sym><type>Tour stops</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="49.461000" lon="-1.306000"><name>DZC Azeville Battery</name><desc>Tour day 1

Four casemates linked by tunnels, painted to look like houses; fired on Utah and held out until 9 June.

Walkable tunnels; pair with Crisbecq.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Blue</sym><type>Tour stops</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="49.337300" lon="-0.460000"><name>DZC Juno Beach Centre — Courseulles</name><desc>Tour day 4

The Canadians took the heaviest losses of the British beaches in the first hour, then advanced further inland than any other force on D-Day.

Canada's museum on the beach; German bunkers in the dunes.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Blue</sym><type>Tour stops</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="49.303730" lon="-0.450570"><name>DZC Bény-sur-Mer Canadian Cemetery</name><desc>Tour day 4

2,048 Canadian graves from Juno and the fighting for Caen.

Quiet, rarely crowded.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Blue</sym><type>Tour stops</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="49.265280" lon="-0.310030"><name>DZC Hillman fortress — Colleville-Montgomery</name><desc>Tour day 5

The German regimental HQ bunker complex that held up the Suffolks for most of D-Day and helped stop Sword's advance on Caen.

Restored bunkers; open on set days.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Blue</sym><type>Tour stops</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="49.285700" lon="-0.255300"><name>DZC Sword Beach — Ouistreham</name><desc>Tour day 5

Lord Lovat's commandos landed here with a piper playing and marched to relieve Pegasus Bridge by early afternoon. The Grand Bunker — the German fire-control tower — is now a museum.

Grand Bunker museum and the Kieffer commando memorial.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html

DZ Charlie field guides · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/normandy.html"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Blue</sym><type>Tour stops</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="49.232500" lon="-0.258500"><name>DZC Ranville War Cemetery</name><desc>Tour day 5

2,236 graves, mostly of the airborne division, beside the first village liberated by the British.

Next to Ranville church; free.

Accuracy: Documented site.

